<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT ONE LINE OF A PLAN LOOKS LIKE - a step, a sentence, a failure with the way out under
        it, and a command somebody could type instead. Templates and styles only, not one value.

        IT ARRIVED ON 2026-09-07 AND THE MARKUP RATCHET IS WHAT ASKED - the second time in five days
        that this ceiling has made somebody look for a seam before looking for room, and the second
        time the seam was already there.

        NO ORDINAL IN THAT SENTENCE, ON PURPOSE. Every file in this directory that carried one is
        wrong by now: Plan.xaml still calls itself the twelfth and there are fifteen, and CLAUDE.md
        said thirteen when there were fourteen. A count of files is one `ls` away and a count
        written into prose only ever ages. Plan.xaml was created on 2026-09-02
        by moving these templates out of PlanView.xaml, and its opening sentence named two subjects
        at once: what a plan is MADE OF on screen, and the sheet it is made of it IN. Adding the
        failure template took that file to 445 lines against 376, so the two subjects became two
        files.

        THE SPLIT IS BY SUBJECT AND NOT BY LENGTH, which is what makes it survive: everything here
        is drawn once per ITEM by an ItemsControl, and everything in Plan.xaml is drawn once per
        SHEET. That is also the practical difference - a template here is instantiated as many
        times as the plan has steps, so what it costs is multiplied and what the sheet costs is not.

        MERGED AFTER Controls.xaml AND BEFORE PlanOffers.xaml AND Plan.xaml, and none of it is
        optional: StaticResource resolves WHILE A FILE IS BEING READ, so a dictionary merged after
        this one is a name that does not exist yet - and PlanOffers.xaml extends the copy button
        declared here. App.xaml carries the order and three other places repeat it: WpfHost in the
        tests, tools/wpfui-probe, and the pool in AppearanceGuards.

        THE LINES WITH A BUTTON UNDER THEM LEFT FOR PlanOffers.xaml ON 2026-09-24 - a failure with
        "Force stop..." and a warning with "Also stop it". That file says why.

        NOTHING HERE BINDS A Click, and that is a rule rather than an oversight - a resource
        dictionary has no code-behind, so a handler named here would resolve against nothing. The
        copy buttons below travel their line in Tag instead, and PlanView.xaml catches the press by
        bubbling, where the markup is compiled with a class that can hold the method.
    -->

    <!--
        ONE STEP. The number is part of the sentence rather than a column, because a plan is read
        as prose - "3. stop Netlogon (would break otherwise)" - and a person counting steps wants
        to see where they are in the order.

        WHAT SOMEBODY ASKED FOR READS DIFFERENTLY FROM WHAT COMES ALONG, and `C2` is why: the
        plan warns that stopping three drags seven along, so the seven have to be findable at a
        glance. Subdued goes to the ones nobody asked for, which is the right way round.

        THE ENTRY'S NAME IS SET APART SINCE 2026-09-24, owner's decision: ten lines of "stop" read
        as one grey block with the names lost in it. Three runs, the middle one wearing
        NameInSentence - the colour stays the line's, so asked and along still read differently.
        THE THREE RUNS STAND ON ONE LINE WITH NOTHING BETWEEN THEM, and that is not tidiness:
        whitespace between inlines in markup is a space on screen, and the spaces the sentence
        needs are already in its parts. Mode=OneWay because a Run's Text binds both ways by
        default and these parts cannot be written back. The automation name is bound to the
        whole line because a TextBlock of runs answers Text with nothing - PlanView.xaml says it
        at the title, where it was measured.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanLineTemplate">
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Text}"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
            <TextBlock.Style>
                <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource PlanStepText}">
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Asked}" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </TextBlock.Style><Run Text="{Binding Sentence.Before, Mode=OneWay}" /><Run Text="{Binding Sentence.Name, Mode=OneWay}" Style="{StaticResource NameInSentence}" /><Run Text="{Binding Sentence.After, Mode=OneWay}" /></TextBlock>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        ONE SENTENCE IN A LIST OF THEM: a warning, a refusal, or something that did not work.

        IT NAMED NO COLOUR UNTIL 2026-09-02 AND WAS THEREFORE DRAWN IN SYSTEM BLACK. The implicit
        TextBlock style sets a face and a size and no Foreground, so a TextBlock that names neither
        gets the framework's default - which is black. On this panel's #343434 that is a contrast
        ratio of **1.69**, against the 4.5 WCAG asks. The owner's words were that the panel is black
        text on grey and that nothing is visible in it, and a scan of the capture agreed exactly:
        in the band holding this sentence NOT ONE PIXEL was brighter than the background.

        MY OWN EYE READ THE SAME PICTURE AS LIGHT GREY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS WRITTEN DOWN. The
        glyphs are black and their antialiased edges are not, so at a glance the mass reads as grey
        - `docs/11` already records that at these ratios you cannot tell "the colour is not
        arriving" from "the colour arrives and nobody sees it". The instrument settles it, not the
        eye, and the same fault is recorded beside HeadingText in Themes/Text.xaml from 2026-09-01.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanSentenceTemplate">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        A COMMAND LINE, IN THE FIXED WIDTH FACE. `docs/03` part 4 asks for it wherever two
        different things must not be able to look the same, and a command somebody is about to
        paste into a terminal is exactly that - one wrong character is a different service.

        THE FACE IS ASKED FOR BY STYLE RATHER THAN BY SETTER SINCE 2026-09-02, and that is the same
        repair as the sentence above rather than tidying: this named the face and not the colour, so
        the one thing on the panel a person is meant to COPY was drawn in system black at 1.69
        against the surface under it. FixedText names both, and has named both since the day another
        style was caught with the same hole.
    -->
    <!--
        THE RECESS A COMMAND SITS IN. Darker than the sheet, one point of edge, and padded on both
        sides - the argument for the fill is beside SurfaceCommandBox in Surfaces.xaml.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanCommandBox" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceCommandBox}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource CornerTile}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadChip}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginChip}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE BUTTON THAT TAKES IT AWAY. Quiet on purpose: it stands beside a command in a fixed face,
        and a filled button there would be the loudest thing in a block whose subject is the text.
        An outline says "control" without saying "press me before you read this".

        IT KEEPS ITS SIZE WHEN THE WORD CHANGES. Copy becomes Copied for two seconds, and a box that
        grew by two characters would shift the command beside it every time somebody pressed it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanCopyButton" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadChip}" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource WidthCopyButton}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceChipEdge}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <!--
                            THE PANEL'S HOVER SINCE 2026-09-16: the window's read 1.30 on this sheet,
                            so pointing at "Copy" showed nothing. Argument beside the brush.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHoverOnPanel}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE BUTTON THAT TAKES EVERY COMMAND OF A SECTION AT ONCE, 2026-09-16 - the owner's remark
        over a sheet with five commands and five buttons that each took one. The same quiet outline
        as the button beside a line, standing at the right end of the section's heading, and it is
        only there when there is more than one line to take (Planned.HasSeveralCommands). Its Tag
        carries the lines already joined, so the press bubbles to the same handler as its siblings.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanCopyAllButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource PlanCopyButton}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE BOX WITH NOTHING DRAWN, 2026-09-16 - the owner's remark that "Copy all" stood further
        right than the "Copy" of every line under it. It did, by exactly the box: a line's button sits
        inside PlanCommandBox, behind its margin, its edge and its padding, and the heading's button
        sat in the heading with none of them. GUI rule 14 forbids a margin found by measuring the gap,
        so the heading's button stands in the same box with its paint taken off - the same three
        tokens, one BasedOn, and the two right edges are one number whatever those tokens become.
        PlanViewGuards asks for the two edges, not for the tokens.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanCommandGhost" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource PlanCommandBox}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{x:Null}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{x:Null}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        AND SINCE 2026-09-02 IT HAS A WAY OFF THE SCREEN, WHICH IS THE OWNER'S FIRST REMARK OF THE
        SECOND SERIES. The command was a sentence somebody had to retype by hand, one character at
        a time, into a terminal where one wrong character is a different service - the exact risk
        the fixed-width face was chosen to reduce, left standing in the one place it mattered most.

        A BOX PER COMMAND RATHER THAN ONE BUTTON FOR THE BLOCK. A plan over several picked rows
        prints one line each, and copying all of them at once is a different thing from copying the
        one somebody is about to run.

        THE BUTTON IS ALWAYS THERE RATHER THAN ON HOVER. A control that appears under the pointer is
        prettier and invisible to anybody working from the keyboard, and it is invisible to anybody
        who does not already know it is there - which is everybody, the first time.

        IT STOPPED WRAPPING AND STARTED SCROLLING. A long command used to grow the sheet downwards
        and move the button under it, which is how a person aims at one thing and presses another.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanCommandTemplate">
        <Border Style="{StaticResource PlanCommandBox}">
            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <!--
                    THE WHEEL IS NOT THIS VIEWER'S TO KEEP - the owner's remark of 2026-09-16 over a
                    sheet of twenty commands: "the scroll stops working". A ScrollViewer marks every
                    wheel event handled whether or not it has anywhere to go, and this one scrolls
                    sideways only - so with command boxes under the pointer the sheet behind them
                    never heard the wheel. NOTHING HERE CAN SAY OTHERWISE, and two doors were tried:
                    the framework's own switch for it, HandlesMouseWheelScrolling, is not settable
                    from markup (MC3072, the same day), and a viewer of our own that ignores the
                    wheel cannot be named from a theme file, `docs/10` trap 10. So the sheet takes
                    the wheel BEFORE this viewer sees it: PlanView.xaml catches PreviewMouseWheel on
                    its body, which tunnels through here first, and raises the wheel on the body
                    itself. The bar and the keys still scroll a long command sideways.
                -->
                <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="0"
                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                              VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" Style="{StaticResource FixedText}" />
                </ScrollViewer>

                <!--
                    THE COMMAND TRAVELS ON THE BUTTON ITSELF, in Tag, because an ItemsControl builds
                    one of these per line and whoever handles the press has to know WHICH. Reading
                    the selection, or the DataContext of whatever had focus, is how the wrong line
                    gets copied.

                    AND THERE IS NO Click HERE, WHICH IS PULAPKA 10 ARRIVING IN A NEW SHAPE. A theme
                    file is a plain dictionary with no code behind it, so a handler named here would
                    not resolve against anything. The press is caught by PlanView.xaml instead, on
                    the ItemsControl above this template, where Click arrives by bubbling and the
                    markup is compiled with a class that can hold the method.
                -->
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Style="{StaticResource PlanCopyButton}"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planCopyCommand"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.plan.copy}"
                        Content="{DynamicResource gui.plan.copy}"
                        ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.copy.hint}"
                        Tag="{Binding}" />
            </Grid>
        </Border>
    </DataTemplate>
</ResourceDictionary>
